\section{Translators}{

The \code{translator} argument chooses how names, and the pseudo-classes
whose meaning depends on document semantics, are translated:
\itemize{
\item \code{"generic"} — plain CSS and XPath semantics: element and attribute
names are matched case-sensitively, and no pseudo-class carries an
HTML-specific meaning.
\item \code{"html"} — lower-cases element and attribute names, as HTML parsing
does, compares the values of the attributes HTML makes
case-insensitive (\code{type}, \code{rel}, \code{lang}, …) without regard to case,
and gives \verb{:link}, \verb{:any-link}, \verb{:checked}, \verb{:default}, \verb{:enabled},
\verb{:disabled} (including HTML's \code{fieldset}/\code{legend} carve-out),
\verb{:required}, \verb{:optional}, \verb{:read-write}, \verb{:read-only},
\verb{:placeholder-shown} and \verb{:lang()} their static HTML meaning. Most
of them are confined to the elements HTML defines them over, so a
selector naming any other element never matches: \code{"a:enabled"}
translates to \code{"a[0]"}. The exceptions are \verb{:lang()} and the
\verb{:read-write}/\verb{:read-only} pair, which every element answers.
\item \code{"xhtml"} — the same pseudo-class meanings as \code{"html"}, but names
and attribute values keep their case, XHTML being XML, and \verb{:lang()}
reads \code{xml:lang} in preference to \code{lang}.
}

The argument is matched case-insensitively and on a unique prefix, so
\code{"g"}, \code{"H"} and \code{"xh"} name the \code{"generic"}, \code{"html"} and \code{"xhtml"}
translators.
}

\section{Supported selectors}{

Every translator handles:
\itemize{
\item Type, universal (\code{*}) and namespaced (\code{"ns|e"}, \code{"*|e"}, \code{"|e"})
selectors.
\item ID (\code{"#id"}) and class (\code{".class"}) selectors.
\item Attribute selectors — \verb{[attr]}, \code{=}, \verb{~=}, \verb{|=}, \verb{^=}, \verb{$=} and
\verb{*=} — with the Level 4 \code{i} and \code{s} case-sensitivity flags.
\item The descendant, child (\code{>}), next-sibling (\code{+}) and
subsequent-sibling (\code{~}) combinators, and selector lists
(\code{"a, b"}).
\item The nth family — \verb{:nth-child()}, \verb{:nth-last-child()},
\verb{:nth-of-type()}, \verb{:nth-last-of-type()}, \verb{:first-child},
\verb{:last-child}, \verb{:first-of-type}, \verb{:last-of-type}, \verb{:only-child}
and \verb{:only-of-type} — including the Level 4 \verb{An+B of S} form.
\item \verb{:is()} (and its legacy alias \verb{:matches()}), \verb{:where()}, \verb{:not()}
and \verb{:has()}, with complex, combinator-bearing arguments and with a
leading combinator inside \verb{:has()} (\code{"e:has(> .foo)"}).
\item \verb{:scope}, \verb{:root}, \verb{:empty} and \verb{:lang()}.
}

The pseudo-classes whose meaning rests on user or runtime state that
a static document does not have are parsed but never match,
translating to the always-false predicate \verb{[0]}: \verb{:hover}, \verb{:active},
\verb{:focus}, \verb{:focus-within}, \verb{:focus-visible}, \verb{:visited}, \verb{:target},
\verb{:target-within}, \verb{:local-link} and \verb{:dir()}, plus — under the
\code{"generic"} translator, which has no HTML semantics to give them —
\verb{:link}, \verb{:any-link}, \verb{:checked}, \verb{:default}, \verb{:enabled},
\verb{:disabled}, \verb{:required}, \verb{:optional}, \verb{:read-write}, \verb{:read-only}
and \verb{:placeholder-shown}.

Everything else is an error rather than an approximation:
\itemize{
\item Pseudo-elements (\code{"::before"}, \code{"::slotted()"}, \code{"::part()"}).
\item The column combinator and \verb{:nth-col()} / \verb{:nth-last-col()}, and the
of-type pseudo-classes without an element type to count with, as
described above.
\item The non-standard \verb{[attr!=value]} and \verb{:contains()}.
\item Any other pseudo-class, including the form pseudo-classes \verb{:valid},
\verb{:in-range} and \verb{:indeterminate}, whose meaning no static
translation can reach, so that a typo stays loud instead of
silently matching nothing.
\item \verb{:host}, the \code{&} nesting selector, and a \verb{:has()} nested inside
another \verb{:has()}.
\item \verb{:scope} outside the leftmost compound, or inside a functional
pseudo-class such as \code{":is(:scope)"}.
}
}

\section{Errors}{

Errors raised by selectrs are classed conditions, so callers can tell
the kinds of failure apart without matching on the message. All of them
inherit from \code{selectrs_error}, and each carries the fields listed here:
\itemize{
\item \code{selectrs_parse_error} — the selector is not valid CSS. Fields
\code{selector}, \code{index} (which element of a vectorised call failed) and
\code{column}, the 1-based character column the parse failed at.
\item \code{selectrs_translation_error} — the selector is valid CSS but uses a
construct that has no XPath 1.0 equivalent. Fields \code{selector},
\code{index}, \code{construct}, and \code{column} when the construct could be
located in the selector. Some constructs are only recognised once
the selector has been parsed, and those have no \code{column}.
\item \code{selectrs_argument_error} — the arguments themselves are wrong (a
non-character selector, an \code{NA}, a selector or prefix that is not
valid in its declared encoding, an unknown translator, lengths that
do not recycle).
}

The \code{selector} field holds the UTF-8 translation of the element that
failed, which is the string that was actually translated, not the
caller's original object.

Every one of these classes is signalled under its selectr name as
well - \code{selectr_parse_error}, \code{selectr_translation_error},
\code{selectr_argument_error} and \code{selectr_error} - so a handler,
\code{inherits()} test or \code{expect_error(class = )} written against selectr
fires here unchanged. The selectrs names come first, so \code{class(e)[1]}
and the printed header still name the package that raised the error.

selectr's names for two of the fields are carried alongside the ones
above, for the same reason: \code{pos} on any condition that has a \code{column}
(the same number), and \code{feature} on a translation error (the same
string as \code{construct}). \code{column}, \code{index} and \code{construct} are the
primary names. Note that \code{feature}'s \emph{value} is not the one selectr
would give — selectr names the construct with a short token such as
\code{":scope"}, where selectrs describes it in a phrase — so a handler
that compares \code{feature} to a literal needs rewriting even though one
that prints it does not.
}

